WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.640
As you begin to look into healthcare analytics,&nbsp;
you may find yourself searching for definitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.120
and differences between health informatics,&nbsp;
bioinformatics and healthcare analytics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.800
It’s not hard to see why, as the three&nbsp;
disciplines work in similar areas of the health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.160
computing and IT space. But, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.240
they are very different and have uniquely&nbsp;
defined roles that complement one another.&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.360
Health analytics and informatics are both&nbsp;
essential for the success of healthcare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.480
organizations. Healthcare organizations&nbsp;
must be able to manage, analyze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.880
and interpret data in order to identify&nbsp;
the best ways to deliver high quality care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.920
Although some consider analytics and&nbsp;
informatics to be interchangeable terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.880
the two are distinctively&nbsp;
different in concept and practice.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.600
Health informatics is the interdisciplinary&nbsp;
study of the design, development, adoption, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.560
application of IT-based innovations in healthcare&nbsp;
services delivery, management, and planning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.400
In practical terms, healthcare informatics&nbsp;
relates to the ecosystem of data and systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.280
that support operational and clinical workflows&nbsp;
rather than the analysis of data found therein.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.960
The diagram depicts the relationship between&nbsp;
the 2 major domain of health informatics:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.680
Health Information Technology which focuses on&nbsp;
the tools and machines, and Health Information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:33.200
Management, focuses on the organization and&nbsp;
dissemination of information. Example of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:39.840
job scope of a health informaticist may include
Managing EHR integration into clinical workflows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.240
Reduce health disparities,&nbsp;
Engage patients and families,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.280
Improve care coordination, Maintain&nbsp;
privacy of health information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.640
Improve access to health information,&nbsp;
and Improve education and compliance.&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.200
Healthcare analytics, on the other hand, refers&nbsp;
to analysis of the data to inform decisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.320
improve quality and performance&nbsp;
of the healthcare organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.480
Health data analysts have the advanced knowledge&nbsp;
to acquire, manage, analyze, interpret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:19.040
and transform data into accurate, consistent, and&nbsp;
timely information. Example of the job scope of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.800
health data analyst may include Increase access to&nbsp;
care and decrease costs using business analytics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.640
Overcome re-imbursement&nbsp;
challenges using smart algorithm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:35.840
Improve patient outcomes using big data analytics,&nbsp;
Optimize resources using descriptive analytics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:42.800
Conduct research using advanced analytics, Improve&nbsp;
clinical quality using operational analytics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:50.080
and Detect fraud using prescriptive&nbsp;
and real-time analytics.&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.320
Another field of prominent importance in&nbsp;
the field of analytics and informatics is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:02.960
bioinformatics. Bioinformatics focus on analysis,&nbsp;
interpretation, data mining, and integration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:10.080
of heterogeneous ‘omics’ data, including the&nbsp;
genome, transcriptome , proteome, metabolome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:17.680
and algorithmic modeling of biological&nbsp;
systems. It has two roles. As a technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:23.680
bioinfomatics is a powerful technology to manage,&nbsp;
search, and analyze big data in life sciences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:30.800
As a methodology, bioinfomatics is a top-down&nbsp;
holistic, data-driven, genome-wide, and systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:36.240
approach that generates new hypotheses, finds new&nbsp;
patterns, and discovers new functional elements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.560
It complements traditional experimental&nbsp;
biology methods. A seamless combination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.600
of computational and experimental methods should&nbsp;
be the best way to study a biological question.&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:56.320
The Bio in bioinformatics signifies the biological&nbsp;
questions it studies, many of which can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.880
grouped under the conceptual framework from&nbsp;
genotype to phenotype and the Central Dogma.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.040
Some examples may help you understand&nbsp;
better. For instance, sequence alignment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:14.160
Are the sequences of two genes or proteins&nbsp;
similar? Could they be homologous? How can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.400
we find the closest homologue of the gene&nbsp;
we are studying from the vast databases?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.520
Can I use the known function of a known gene&nbsp;
to guide the study of my gene of interest?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:30.880
Given DNA and genome sequences, how can we find&nbsp;
the genes in the vast genome? How can we compare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:36.000
the similarity and difference between two whole&nbsp;
genomes and reconstruct the evolutionary history?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:42.400
What are the syntenic regions between two&nbsp;
genomes? How can we identify which genomic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:48.320
regions are methylated? At the level of RNA&nbsp;
expression, we often need to find out which genes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:53.360
are differentially expressed between two organs&nbsp;
or tissues, between tumor and normal tissues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:59.280
or between two different developmental stages?&nbsp;
At the level of proteins, how do you identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:05.280
the expressed proteins from mass spec data?&nbsp;
Proteins do not exist in a linear form in nature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.240
Instead, they are folded into beautiful&nbsp;
three dimensional structures in nature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:15.120
How can we predict the three-dimensional&nbsp;
structure of a protein from its sequence?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:22.480
Molecules do not exist or function in isolation.&nbsp;
Instead, they form complex molecular network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.880
How can we construct protein-protein interaction&nbsp;
networks, transcription regulation networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.240
and metabolic and signaling pathways? What&nbsp;
are the dynamic features of these networks?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.680
Furthermore, with these data and researches,&nbsp;
how can we simulate a virtual cell?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:45.840
Last but not least, how do you compare different&nbsp;
people’s genomes and use population genetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.720
approaches to compare the genomes of the ill&nbsp;
and the healthy, and identify the gene mutations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.840
that cause diseases? From these examples,&nbsp;
we can see that there are many interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:00.320
and important questions in life sciences&nbsp;
that await our bioinformatics investigation.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:08.560
Seeing bioinformatics from another angle,&nbsp;
the informatics in Bioinformatics signifies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:12.880
the information and computational methods&nbsp;
to manage, search, and analyze the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:19.600
The theme runs along the axis from data&nbsp;
to discovery. First, the storage, index,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.000
and search of terabytes to petabytes&nbsp;
of big data require advanced databases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:28.880
Just imagine, your laptop probably&nbsp;
has several Gigabytes of hard disk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:34.640
However just one set of our experiments often&nbsp;
generates one or more terabytes of data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:41.200
which is too large to fit in your laptop. Managing&nbsp;
such big data requires advanced database systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:46.480
Analysis of noisy big data requires&nbsp;
the development of many algorithms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:52.400
software tools, and web servers. These make&nbsp;
up a big portion of bioinformatics research.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:57.680
A tradition in bioinformatics since the&nbsp;
early days till today has been to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:03.440
most algorithms and software open source. This&nbsp;
has contributed significantly to the broader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:09.840
life science research, and had helped to advance&nbsp;
bioinformatics itself as well. With these tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.240
we can do a lot of data mining to discover&nbsp;
new patterns and phenomena in life science.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.880
Finally, by integration of data and tools, we can&nbsp;
build predictive models of biological systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:26.560
As more and more high-throughput technologies&nbsp;
are used more and more in life science, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.360
will continue to be numerous new opportunities&nbsp;
for technology innovation in bioinformatics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:36.080
To meet this challenge, a new breed of&nbsp;
professional that could collaborate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.880
communicate with biomedical researchers,&nbsp;
clinicians, quantitative scientists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.520
bioethicists is needed to translate the&nbsp;
genomic knowledge to improve patient outcomes.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:54.080
In summary, healthcare analytics, health&nbsp;
informatics or bioinformatics are basically tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:59.760
transform raw biological, patient and prescription&nbsp;
data into meaningful and useful information used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:04.800
to enable more effective strategic, tactical,&nbsp;
operational insights, and decision-making.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:10.720
Data is now available more digitally as technical&nbsp;
advances have made it easier to collect and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:16.560
analyze information from multiple sources. The&nbsp;
government, regulatory agency and other public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.560
stakeholders have been opening their vast&nbsp;
stores of healthcare knowledge, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.840
data from clinical trials and information on&nbsp;
patients covered under public registry programs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.360
In parallel pharmaceutical companies have been&nbsp;
aggregating years of research and development data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:36.560
into medical databases, while payors and providers&nbsp;
have digitized their patient records in EHR.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:42.080
As healthcare becomes a greater portion of&nbsp;
expenses for employers, public, private,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:46.800
and patients, there is an increasing focus&nbsp;
on analytics to drive efficiency and value.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:52.160
Therefore, it is important that the best&nbsp;
data analytics and informatics professionals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:56.800
collaborate on transforming the way data is&nbsp;
gathered, maintained, and analyzed through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.640
technology solutions. Healthcare analyst,&nbsp;
health informaticist and bioinformatician&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:07.360
need to integrate and collaborate with clinicians&nbsp;
and experts who understand the healthcare system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.680
and use data analytics, and leveraging the&nbsp;
strengths of each profession effectively.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.120
Working as a team, informatics and analytics&nbsp;
professionals are bridging the gaps and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:20.640
introducing action-oriented improvement&nbsp;
opportunities with the intention and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:32.640
potential of improving healthcare safety,&nbsp;
quality, efficiency, and cost of care.

